From: NimRTC contributors <bot@nimrtc.local>
Subject: [PATCH] libjuice: a TURN-over-TLS transport seam, without a plaintext
 fallback

Upstream base commit: 77daa8befd828046169adbb012d8de928bfdcdd4

Applies on top of: the NimRTC libjuice tree after PR #34 (TURN over TCP,
which added src/turn_tcp.c) and PR #50 (0001-so-reuseaddr).  It does not
apply to a pristine upstream checkout.

Upstream libjuice knows the TURN transport enum value
JUICE_TURN_TRANSPORT_TLS but has no way to actually speak it: the
allocation path logs "TURN over TLS is not supported, falling back to
TCP" and opens a *plain* TCP control connection to the server.  That
fallback is the defect this patch removes.  A TURN server reached over
TLS expects the client to speak TURN inside a TLS session; a plain TCP
connection to the same port either fails (a TLS-only listener) or, on a
server that also listens in the clear, silently sends the TURN
credentials in the clear.

libjuice owns the TURN protocol and its self-delimiting stream framing
(RFC 8656 sections 12.4 / 12.5), but it does not own a TLS stack, and a
vendored MPL-2.0 tree is the wrong place to carry one.  So the host
supplies the TLS client through a narrow ops seam, and libjuice uses it
for the *control connection* only.  The relayed transport address
allocated by the server stays UDP, as RFC 6062 requires.

The seam is juice_turn_tls_ops_t in include/juice/juice.h:

  - connect  wraps an already-connected socket in a TLS session
  - handshake advances the handshake, one step at a time
  - read     / write transfer bytes
  - close    releases the session

The implementation must be stream oriented: a write may transfer fewer
bytes than asked for, and a read may return fewer bytes than requested.
libjuice's framing is self-delimiting, so partial messages are handled
by the caller.

The ops are consulted for JUICE_TURN_TRANSPORT_TLS only.  A NULL ops
pointer, or a NULL connect, means "TLS is not available", and the TURN
entry then fails immediately: it does NOT fall back to plain TCP.  The
agent_turn_state_t::failed flag records that, so the entry fails at
once instead of waiting for the retransmission timeout.

The connection itself becomes three-phase, because a TLS session can
only be created once the TCP connection has completed, and the
handshake may need several round trips:

  1. the non-blocking TCP connect, as before
  2. the TLS session, created lazily from the connected socket
  3. the TLS handshake, advanced incrementally

Once the session exists, turn_tcp_stream_recv() and flush_locked() go
through the TLS ops rather than recv()/send() on the raw socket.  The
session owns the socket from that point on, and closes it.

The public juice_turn_server_t gains a server_name field: the name
presented as SNI and verified against the server's certificate.  NULL
means "use the host name", which is the right default when the host
name is also the name in the certificate.

NimRTC's host side of the seam is src/modules/ice/src/turn_tls.cpp,
which implements the ops on top of wolfSSL (TLS 1.2, CA verification,
SAN dNSName check, SNI).
diff --git a/include/juice/juice.h b/include/juice/juice.h
index 34a0807c..f95aa55e 100644
--- a/include/juice/juice.h
+++ b/include/juice/juice.h
@@ -93,12 +93,83 @@ typedef enum juice_turn_transport {
 	JUICE_TURN_TRANSPORT_TLS      // TURN over TLS (RFC 8656 section 12)
 } juice_turn_transport_t;
 
+// The TLS transport seam (NimRTC extension, see
+// src/third_party/libjuice/patches/0002-turn-tls-transport.patch).
+//
+// libjuice owns the TURN protocol but not a TLS stack: it would have to
+// carry one in the vendored tree, and a vendored MPL-2.0 tree is the
+// wrong place for it.  The host instead supplies the TLS client, and
+// libjuice uses it for the *control connection* only.
+//
+// The contract is deliberately narrow:
+//
+//   - `connect` wraps an already-connected socket in a TLS session and
+//     returns a session handle, or NULL on failure.  It must not block
+//     indefinitely: the caller polls it.
+//
+//   - `handshake` advances the handshake.  It returns 1 when the
+//     session is established, 0 when it still needs to make progress
+//     (the caller should wait for readability and retry), and -1 on
+//     failure.
+//
+//   - `read` / `write` transfer bytes.  They return the number of bytes
+//     transferred, 0 when the operation would block, and -1 on failure.
+//
+// The implementation must be **stream oriented**: a `write` of N bytes
+// may transfer fewer, and a `read` may return fewer bytes than
+// requested.  libjuice's framing is self-delimiting (RFC 8656 sections
+// 12.4 / 12.5), so partial messages are handled by the caller.
+//
+// The ops are only consulted for JUICE_TURN_TRANSPORT_TLS.  A NULL ops
+// pointer, or a NULL `connect`, means "TLS is not available", and the
+// TURN entry then fails to allocate rather than silently falling back
+// to plain TCP -- a silent downgrade is the defect this seam exists to
+// prevent.
+typedef struct juice_turn_tls_ops {
+	void *ctx;
+
+	// Wrap an already-connected socket in a TLS session.
+	//
+	// `socket_fd` is a connected, non-blocking TCP socket.  The
+	// implementation takes ownership of it: on success the session
+	// owns the socket, and on failure the implementation must close it.
+	//
+	// `host` is the host name that was resolved (for diagnostics and
+	// as the default certificate name), `port` the server port, and
+	// `server_name` the name to present as SNI and to verify the
+	// certificate against.  `server_name` may be empty, in which case
+	// `host` is used.
+	void *(*connect)(void *ctx, int socket_fd, const char *host, uint16_t port,
+	                 const char *server_name);
+
+	// Advance the handshake.  1 = established, 0 = pending, -1 = failed.
+	int (*handshake)(void *session);
+
+	// Read up to `size` bytes.  Returns the count, 0 if it would block,
+	// or -1 on failure.
+	int (*read)(void *session, char *buf, size_t size);
+
+	// Write up to `size` bytes.  Returns the count, 0 if it would block,
+	// or -1 on failure.
+	int (*write)(void *session, const char *buf, size_t size);
+
+	// Close and release the session.
+	void (*close)(void *session);
+} juice_turn_tls_ops_t;
+
 typedef struct juice_turn_server {
 	const char *host;
 	const char *username;
 	const char *password;
 	uint16_t port;
 	juice_turn_transport_t transport;
+
+	// The name presented as SNI and verified against the server's
+	// certificate when `transport` is JUICE_TURN_TRANSPORT_TLS.
+	//
+	// NULL means "use `host`", which is the right default when the
+	// host name is also the name in the certificate.
+	const char *server_name;
 } juice_turn_server_t;
 
 typedef enum juice_concurrency_mode {
@@ -116,6 +187,12 @@ typedef struct juice_config {
 	juice_turn_server_t *turn_servers;
 	int turn_servers_count;
 
+	// The TLS client used for TURN-over-TLS control connections.
+	// NULL (the default) means TURN-over-TLS is unavailable: a TURN
+	// server configured with JUICE_TURN_TRANSPORT_TLS then fails to
+	// allocate instead of silently downgrading to plain TCP.
+	const juice_turn_tls_ops_t *turn_tls_ops;
+
 	const char *bind_address;
 
 	uint16_t local_port_range_begin;
